1
00:00:00,957 --> 00:00:06,718
section 2 Chapter 9 B When he woke it was with the sensation

2
00:00:06,718 --> 00:00:10,680
of having slept for a long time, but to glance at the old-fashioned clock

3
00:00:10,680 --> 00:00:15,297
told him that it was only twenty Thirty He lay dozing for a while;

4
00:00:15,297 --> 00:00:20,099
then the usual deep lunged singing struck up from the yard below:

5
00:00:20,099 --> 00:00:25,979
It was only an hopeless fancy, It passed like an April die But a look and a word

6
00:00:25,979 --> 00:00:32,878
in the dreams they stood They have stolen my heart away The dribbling song seems to have kept

7
00:00:32,878 --> 00:00:34,137
its popularity.

8
00:00:34,137 --> 00:00:38,279
You still heard it all over the place. It had outlived the Hate Song.

9
00:00:38,279 --> 00:00:43,200
Julia woke at the sound, stretched herself luxuriously and got out of bed.

10
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:46,259
'I'm hungry,' she said. 'Let's make some more coffee.

11
00:00:46,259 --> 00:00:49,500
Damn! The stove's gone out and the water's cold.'

12
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:51,180
She picked the stove up and shook it.

13
00:00:51,180 --> 00:00:53,279
'There's no oil in it.'

14
00:00:53,279 --> 00:00:58,740
'We can get some from old Charrington, I expect funny thing is I made sure it was full.

15
00:00:58,740 --> 00:01:00,840
I'm going to put my clothes on,' she added.

16
00:01:00,840 --> 00:01:03,360
'It seems to have got colder.'

17
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:08,759
Winston also got up and dressed himself in The fatigable voice sang on I saw

18
00:01:08,759 --> 00:01:12,540
that time heals all things, They say you can always forget;

19
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:18,840
But the smiles and the tears across the years They twist my art strings yet!

20
00:01:18,840 --> 00:01:22,919
As he fastens the belt of his overalls he strolled across to the window

21
00:01:22,919 --> 00:01:26,520
sun must have gone down behind the houses; it was not shining into the yard

22
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:31,378
any longer. The flagstones were wet as though they had just been washed, and he

23
00:01:31,378 --> 00:01:34,797
had the feeling that the sky had been washed too, so fresh and pale was the

24
00:01:34,797 --> 00:01:39,479
blue between the chimney pots. Tirelessly the woman marched to and fro Caulking

25
00:01:39,479 --> 00:01:43,259
and uncorking herself, singing and falling silent, and pegging out more

26
00:01:43,259 --> 00:01:45,718
diapers, and more and yet more.

27
00:01:45,718 --> 00:01:49,378
He wondered whether she took in washing for a living, or was merely the slave of

28
00:01:49,378 --> 00:01:51,479
20 or 30 grandchildren.

29
00:01:51,479 --> 00:01:55,619
Julia had come across to his side; together they gazed down with a sort of

30
00:01:55,619 --> 00:01:57,840
fascination of the sturdy figure below.

31
00:01:57,840 --> 00:02:01,619
As he looked at the woman in her characteristic attitude the thick arms

32
00:02:01,619 --> 00:02:05,939
reaching up for the line, her powerful mayor-like buttocks protruded, it struck

33
00:02:05,939 --> 00:02:08,520
him for the first time that she was beautiful.

34
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:12,779
It had never before occurred to him that the body of a woman of 50 blown up to

35
00:02:12,779 --> 00:02:17,340
monstrous dimensions by childbearing, then hardened, roughened by work till it

36
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:21,240
was coarse in the grain like an overripe turn it could be beautiful.

37
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:24,240
But it was so, and after all, he thought, why not?

38
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:29,040
The solid, contourless body, like a block of granite, and the rasping red skin, bore

39
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:32,340
the same relation to the body of a girl as the rosehip to the rose.

40
00:02:32,340 --> 00:02:36,297
Why should the fruit be held inferior to the flower?

41
00:02:36,297 --> 00:02:38,580
'She's beautiful,' he murmured.

42
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:42,119
'She's a meter across the hips, easily,' said Julia.

43
00:02:42,119 --> 00:02:45,599
'That is her style of beauty,' said Winston.

44
00:02:45,599 --> 00:02:49,378
He held Julius supple waste easily encircled by his arm.

45
00:02:49,378 --> 00:02:53,520
From the hip to the knee her flank was against his. Out of their bodies no child

46
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:54,900
would ever come.

47
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:57,599
That was the one thing they could never do.

48
00:02:57,599 --> 00:03:01,740
Only by word of mouth, from mind to mind, could they pass on the secret.

49
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:05,637
The woman down there had no mind, she had only strong arms, a warm heart and a

50
00:03:05,637 --> 00:03:07,020
fertile belly.

51
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:10,020
He wondered how many children she had given birth to.

52
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:14,819
It might easily be 15. She had had her momentary flowering, a year, perhaps, of

53
00:03:14,819 --> 00:03:18,779
Wild Rose beauty, and then she had suddenly swollen like a fertilized fruit

54
00:03:18,779 --> 00:03:22,500
and grown hard and red and coarse, and then her life had been laundering,

55
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:27,297
scrubbing, darning, cooking, sweeping, polishing, mending, scrubbing, laundering,

56
00:03:27,297 --> 00:03:31,979
first for children, then for grandchildren, over 30 unbroken years.

57
00:03:31,979 --> 00:03:34,200
At the end of it she was still singing.

58
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:37,558
The mystical reverence that he felt for her was somehow mixed up with the aspect

59
00:03:37,558 --> 00:03:43,680
of the pale, cloudless sky, stretching away behind the chimney pots into interminable distance

60
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:47,520
It was curious to think that the sky was the same for everybody, in Eurasia or

61
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:49,439
East Asia as well as here.

62
00:03:49,439 --> 00:03:52,619
And the people under the sky were also very much the same—

63
00:03:52,619 --> 00:03:56,218
—everywhere, all over the world, hundreds of thousands of millions of people just

64
00:03:56,218 --> 00:04:00,360
like this, people ignorant of one another's existence, held apart by walls

65
00:04:00,360 --> 00:04:05,400
of hatred and lies, and yet almost exactly the same— —people who had never learned to think

66
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:08,580
but who was soaring up in their hearts and bellies and muscles the power that

67
00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:11,279
would one day overturn the world.

68
00:04:11,279 --> 00:04:13,797
If there was hope, it lay in the proles!

69
00:04:13,797 --> 00:04:19,619
Without having read to the end of the book, he knew that that must be Goldstein's final message.

70
00:04:19,619 --> 00:04:23,580
The future belongs to the pros And could he be sure that when their

71
00:04:23,580 --> 00:04:26,759
time came the world they constructed would not be just as alien to him,

72
00:04:26,759 --> 00:04:30,297
Winston Smith, as the world of the Party?

73
00:04:30,297 --> 00:04:33,840
Yes, because at the least it would be a world of sanity.

74
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:36,479
Where there is equality there can be sanity.

75
00:04:36,479 --> 00:04:40,439
Sooner or later it would happen, strength would change into consciousness. The

76
00:04:40,439 --> 00:04:45,240
pearls were immortal, you could not doubt it when you looked at that valiant figure in the yard.

77
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:47,637
In the end their awakening would come.

78
00:04:47,637 --> 00:04:51,119
And until that happened, though it might be a thousand years, they would stay

79
00:04:51,119 --> 00:04:54,840
alive against all the odds, like birds, passing on from body to body the

80
00:04:54,840 --> 00:04:59,360
vitality which the Party did not share and could not kill.

81
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:03,479
'Do you remember,' he said, 'the thrush that sang to us, that first day, at the edge of

82
00:05:03,479 --> 00:05:04,680
the wood?'

83
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:09,180
'He wasn't singing to us,' said Julia. 'He was singing to please himself. Not even

84
00:05:09,180 --> 00:05:11,939
that. He was just singing.'

85
00:05:11,939 --> 00:05:17,040
The birds sang, the pearls sang, the Party did not sing. All Around the world, in

86
00:05:17,040 --> 00:05:21,000
London and New York, in Africa and Brazil and in the mysterious, forbidden lands

87
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,957
beyond the frontiers, in the streets of Paris and Berlin, in the villages of the

88
00:05:24,957 --> 00:05:29,819
endless Russian plain, in the bazaars of China and Japan— —everywhere stood the

89
00:05:29,819 --> 00:05:34,020
same solid unconquerable figure, made monstrous by work and childbearing,

90
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:38,580
toiling from birth to death and still singing. Out of those mighty loins a race

91
00:05:38,580 --> 00:05:41,340
of conscious beings must one day come.

92
00:05:41,340 --> 00:05:45,957
You were the dead; theirs was the future that you could share in that future if

93
00:05:45,957 --> 00:05:49,860
you kept alive the mind as they kept alive the body, and passed on the secret

94
00:05:49,860 --> 00:05:53,159
doctrine that two plus two make four.

95
00:05:53,159 --> 00:05:55,979
'We are the dead,' he said.

96
00:05:55,979 --> 00:05:59,939
'We are the dead,' echoed Julia dutifully.

97
00:05:59,939 --> 00:06:04,619
'You are the dead see denying voice behind them.

98
00:06:04,619 --> 00:06:09,119
They sprang apart. Winston's entrails seemed to have turned into ice.

99
00:06:09,119 --> 00:06:12,599
He could see the white all around the irises of Julia's eyes.

100
00:06:12,599 --> 00:06:16,797
Her face had turned to milky yellow. The smear of rouge that was still on each

101
00:06:16,797 --> 00:06:22,439
cheekbone stood out sharply, almost as though unconnected with the skin beneath.

102
00:06:22,439 --> 00:06:26,159
'You are the dead,' repeated the iron voice.

103
00:06:26,159 --> 00:06:29,759
'It was behind the picture,' breathed Julia.

104
00:06:29,759 --> 00:06:32,119
'It was behind the picture,' said the voice.

105
00:06:32,119 --> 00:06:34,619
'Remain exactly where you are.

106
00:06:34,619 --> 00:06:38,218
Make no movement until you are ordered.'

107
00:06:38,218 --> 00:06:41,580
It was starting, it was starting at last!

108
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:45,419
They could do nothing except stand gazing into one another's eyes.

109
00:06:45,419 --> 00:06:49,378
To run for life, to get out of the house before it was too late— —no such thought

110
00:06:49,378 --> 00:06:50,878
occurred to them.

111
00:06:50,878 --> 00:06:54,360
Unthinkable to disobey the ion voice from the wall.

112
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:58,919
There was a snap as though a catch had been turned back, and a crash of breaking glass.

113
00:06:58,919 --> 00:07:04,159
The picture had fallen to the floor, uncovering the telescreen behind it

114
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:06,540
and see us,' said Julia.

115
00:07:06,540 --> 00:07:09,297
'Now we can see you,' said the voice.

116
00:07:09,297 --> 00:07:10,797
'Stand out in the middle of the room.

117
00:07:10,797 --> 00:07:18,439
Stand back to back. Clasp your hands behind your heads. Do not touch one another.'

118
00:07:18,479 --> 00:07:22,919
They were not touching, but it seemed to him that he could feel Julia's body shaking.

119
00:07:22,919 --> 00:07:26,939
Or perhaps it was merely the shaking of his own. He could just stop his teeth

120
00:07:26,939 --> 00:07:30,540
from chattering, but his knees were beyond his control.

121
00:07:30,540 --> 00:07:34,860
There was a sound of trampling boots below, inside the house and outside. The

122
00:07:34,860 --> 00:07:36,779
yard seemed to be full of men.

123
00:07:36,779 --> 00:07:40,680
Something was being dragged across the stones. The woman's singing had stopped

124
00:07:40,680 --> 00:07:44,759
abruptly. There was a long, rolling clang, as though the wash tub had been flunked

125
00:07:44,759 --> 00:07:50,599
across the yard, and then a confusion of angry shouts which ended in a yell of pain.

126
00:07:50,637 --> 00:07:53,340
'The house is surrounded,' said Winston.

127
00:07:53,340 --> 00:07:56,457
'The house is surrounded,' said the voice.

128
00:07:56,457 --> 00:07:59,457
He heard Julia snap her teeth together.

129
00:07:59,457 --> 00:08:02,700
'I suppose we may as well say goodbye she said.

130
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:06,058
'You may as well say goodbye said the voice.

131
00:08:06,058 --> 00:08:09,718
And then another quite different voice, a thin, cultivated voice which Winston had

132
00:08:09,718 --> 00:08:15,779
the impression of having heard before, struck in: 'And by the way, while we are on the subject,

133
00:08:15,779 --> 00:08:21,957
"Here comes a candle to light you to bed, here comes a chopper to chop off your head!"'

134
00:08:21,957 --> 00:08:26,040
Something crashed onto the bed behind Winston's back. The head of a ladder had

135
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:29,099
been thrust through the window and had burst in the frame.

136
00:08:29,099 --> 00:08:30,659
Someone was climbing through the window.

137
00:08:30,659 --> 00:08:35,340
There was a stampede of boots up the stairs. The room was full of solid men in

138
00:08:35,340 --> 00:08:41,119
black uniforms, with iron shot boots on their feet and trunches in their hands.

139
00:08:41,297 --> 00:08:43,619
Winston was not trembling any longer.

140
00:08:43,619 --> 00:08:46,200
Even his eyes he barely moved.

141
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:50,878
One thing alone mattered: to keep still, to keep still and not give them an

142
00:08:50,878 --> 00:08:52,378
excuse to hit you!

143
00:08:52,378 --> 00:08:56,580
A man with a smooth prize Fighters jowl in which the mouth was only a slit

144
00:08:56,580 --> 00:09:01,740
paused opposite him, balancing his truncheon meditatively between thumb and forefinger.

145
00:09:01,740 --> 00:09:03,779
Winston met his eyes.

146
00:09:03,779 --> 00:09:07,680
The feeling of nakedness, with one's hands behind one's head and one's face

147
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:11,099
and body all exposed, was almost unbearable.

148
00:09:11,099 --> 00:09:15,000
The man protruded the tip of a white tongue linked to the place where his

149
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,520
lips should have been and then passed on.

150
00:09:17,520 --> 00:09:21,419
There was another crash. Someone had picked up the glass paperweight from the

151
00:09:21,419 --> 00:09:25,378
table and smashed it to pieces on the hearthstone.

152
00:09:25,378 --> 00:09:29,400
The fragment of coral, a tiny crinkle of pink like a sugar rosebud from a cake,

153
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:31,619
rolled across the mat.

154
00:09:31,619 --> 00:09:34,977
How small, thought Winston, how small it always was!

155
00:09:34,977 --> 00:09:38,939
There was a gasp and a thump behind him, and he received a violent kick on the

156
00:09:38,939 --> 00:09:43,439
ankle which nearly flung him off his ballads One of the men had smashed his fist into

157
00:09:43,439 --> 00:09:47,637
Julia's solar plexus, doubling her up like a pocket ruler. She was thrashing

158
00:09:47,637 --> 00:09:49,619
about on the floor, fighting for breath.

159
00:09:49,619 --> 00:09:53,580
Winston and dared not turn his head even by a millimeter but sometimes her liver

160
00:09:53,580 --> 00:09:56,400
to gasping face came within the angle of his vision.

161
00:09:56,400 --> 00:10:00,718
Even in his terror it was as though he could feel the pain in his own body, the

162
00:10:00,718 --> 00:10:05,400
deadly pain which nevertheless was less urgent than the struggle to get back her breath.

163
00:10:05,400 --> 00:10:09,297
He knew what it was like: the terrible, agonizing pain which was

164
00:10:09,297 --> 00:10:13,020
there all the while but could not be suffered yet, because before all else it

165
00:10:13,020 --> 00:10:15,119
was necessary to be able to breathe.

166
00:10:15,119 --> 00:10:19,137
Then two of the men hoisted her up by knees and shoulders and carried her out

167
00:10:19,137 --> 00:10:20,580
of the room like a sack.

168
00:10:20,580 --> 00:10:25,378
Winston had a glimpse of her face, upside down, yellow and contorted, with the eyes

169
00:10:25,378 --> 00:10:28,558
shut, and still with the smear of rouge on either cheek;

170
00:10:28,558 --> 00:10:31,797
and that was the last he saw of her.

171
00:10:31,797 --> 00:10:35,279
He stood dead still. No one had hit him yet.

172
00:10:35,279 --> 00:10:38,580
Thoughts which came of their own accord but seemed totally uninteresting began

173
00:10:38,580 --> 00:10:40,319
to flit through his mind.

174
00:10:40,319 --> 00:10:44,400
He wondered whether they had got Mr tarrington He wondered what they had done to the

175
00:10:44,400 --> 00:10:45,718
woman in the yard.

176
00:10:45,718 --> 00:10:49,080
He noticed that he badly wanted to urinate, and felt a faint surprise,

177
00:10:49,080 --> 00:10:52,378
because he had done so only two or three hours ago.

178
00:10:52,378 --> 00:10:56,580
He noticed that the clock on the mantelpiece said nine, meaning 21 But the

179
00:10:56,580 --> 00:11:02,099
light seemed too strong. Would not the light be fading at 21 hours on an August evening?

180
00:11:02,099 --> 00:11:06,119
He wondered whether after all he and Julia had mistaken the time it slipped

181
00:11:06,119 --> 00:11:10,200
the clock round and thought it was 20 30 when really it was not 8 30 on the

182
00:11:10,200 --> 00:11:12,000
following morning.

183
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:16,680
But he did not pursue the thought further. It was not interesting.

184
00:11:16,680 --> 00:11:20,580
There was another, lighter step in the passage. Mr Charrington came into the

185
00:11:20,580 --> 00:11:25,080
room. The demeanor of the black uniformed men suddenly became more subdued.

186
00:11:25,080 --> 00:11:28,740
Something had also changed in Mr cherrington's appearance.

187
00:11:28,740 --> 00:11:32,340
His eye fell on the fragments of the glass paperweight.

188
00:11:32,340 --> 00:11:35,159
'Pick up those pieces,' he said sharply.

189
00:11:35,159 --> 00:11:40,200
A man stooped to obey. The cockney accent had disappeared; Winston suddenly

190
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:44,457
realized whose voice it was that he had heard a few moments ago on the telescreen.

191
00:11:44,457 --> 00:11:48,599
Mr Charrington was still wearing his old velvet jacket, but his hair, which had

192
00:11:48,599 --> 00:11:51,240
been almost white, had turned black.

193
00:11:51,240 --> 00:11:53,878
Also he was not wearing his spectacles.

194
00:11:53,878 --> 00:11:58,378
He gave Winston a single sharp glance, as though verifying his identity, and then

195
00:11:58,378 --> 00:12:00,477
paid no more attention to him.

196
00:12:00,477 --> 00:12:04,619
He was still recognizable but he was not the same person any longer. His body had

197
00:12:04,619 --> 00:12:07,137
straightened, and seemed to have grown bigger.

198
00:12:07,137 --> 00:12:10,797
His face had undergone only tiny changes that had nevertheless worked to complete

199
00:12:10,797 --> 00:12:15,119
transformation. The black eyebrows were less bushy, the wrinkles were gone, the

200
00:12:15,119 --> 00:12:19,378
whole lines of the face seemed to have altered; even the nose seemed shorter.

201
00:12:19,378 --> 00:12:23,400
It was the alert, cold face of a man of about 5 and 30.

202
00:12:23,400 --> 00:12:26,759
It occurred to Winston that for the first time in his life he was looking,

203
00:12:26,759 --> 00:12:30,000
with knowledge, at a member of the Thought Police
